## 1. Scope
The scope of this strategy is the end-to-end validation of a single-avatar upload and crop capability for authenticated users, including the complete transaction path from front-end visibility controls through gateway routing, synchronous security inspection, object persistence, CDN invalidation, and dynamic UI synchronization. This strategy ingests and preserves the previously supplied scope boundaries and test design parameters without alteration. It covers the specific business need that a user’s public profile identity be updated through a controlled, secure, high-confidence upload mechanism that is visible only to authorized sessions and propagated immediately across active views. The business boundaries explicitly require that upload controls remain hidden or disabled without a fresh valid authentication token and that unauthorized requests be rejected at the gateway with HTTP 401 Unauthorized. These controls are treated as release-blocking behaviors because they define the product’s access model and security posture.

This strategy further includes validation of the hard payload threshold at 5MB, with acceptance at exactly 5.00MB and rejection at 5.01MB and above, along with strict content allow-listing for JPEG and WebP only. The validation obligation extends beyond extension checks to structural header verification through magic-number inspection, which materially reduces the risk of spoofed or malicious payloads entering the processing pipeline. Inline malware scanning is in scope as a synchronous pre-storage gate, meaning the system must complete inspection before persistence to the authorized cloud bucket. The file path must proceed through the API Gateway, and no client-side compression, scaling, or browser-side transformation is permitted. From a quality engineering perspective, this requires cross-layer evidence collection from browser, gateway, scan service, storage, and CDN telemetry so the test program can prove both acceptance flows and reject-path non-events.

Finally, the scope includes end-to-end non-functional verification of the 2.0-second SLA under standard 4G network conditions and immediate cross-view avatar propagation without full page reload. The strategy therefore spans UI component behavior, API and gateway validation, security enforcement, storage correctness, CDN purge event sequencing, and front-end dynamic DOM refresh verification. Out-of-scope areas remain unchanged from the business boundaries: client-side compression tools, local image scaling engines, front-end canvas manipulation scripts, support for PNG/SVG/GIF/raw design formats, concurrent batch uploads, multi-image profile header adjustments, and storage or deployment targets outside the authorized cloud ecosystem. These exclusions are intentionally preserved to avoid quality dilution and to ensure all coverage effort is concentrated on the approved architecture and acceptance criteria for this release.

## 2. Assumptions and Dependencies
This test strategy assumes the staging environment mirrors the authorized cloud ecosystem closely enough to produce representative evidence for gateway routing, synchronous malware scanning, object storage persistence, and CDN invalidation behavior. It also assumes that test accounts can be provisioned with valid, expired, and absent authentication token states, and that the team has controlled access to browser session cookies or equivalent authentication fixtures needed to force those states deterministically. The strategy depends on seeded test assets that include valid JPEG and WebP images, boundary-sized files, unsupported content types, spoofed renamed payloads, and safe malware-simulation artifacts approved for test usage.

The strategy also depends on observability sources being available during execution. Gateway request logs, malware inspection status logs, storage event logs, CDN purge traces, and browser-side network timing evidence are necessary to prove not only successful positive flows but also the absence of downstream events after failed validation. Another key dependency is network shaping capability for standard 4G simulation, because the 2.0-second SLA is meaningful only when the traffic conditions match the stated acceptance criteria. Execution additionally depends on release governance honoring a Human-in-the-Loop approval gate before production-adjacent publication and on development teams preserving API contracts long enough for automated suites to stabilize across the planned execution window.

## 3. Testing Overview

### 3.1 Testing Quality Objectives
The primary quality objective is to establish with high confidence that avatar upload and crop capability operates securely, predictably, and within performance expectations across every required system boundary. The objective is not limited to confirming that a file can be uploaded; it is to prove that the product enforces authenticated access, rejects invalid session states, validates structural content, prevents unsafe persistence, stores only approved files in the designated cloud location, invalidates CDN cache immediately, and refreshes all relevant user-facing surfaces without a page reload. In practical release terms, the feature is acceptable only if it demonstrates data path integrity from UI entry point to final distributed render state. This objective aligns directly with the business requirement that public identity changes be trustworthy and immediately recognizable to other members.

A second major quality objective is to validate resilience around negative and boundary conditions. Exact boundary acceptance at 5.00MB and rejection at 5.01MB are critical because they often expose off-by-one gateway misconfigurations, proxy buffering defects, or mismatched unit conversions between UI and backend components. Likewise, strict MIME and magic-number enforcement are quality objectives because extension-only validation provides a false sense of control and leaves the system exposed to spoofed content. Malware scanning, as a synchronous gate, must be proven to prevent persistence before any storage event occurs. The test program will therefore prioritize reject-path verification just as strongly as success-path validation, with evidence gathered from browser behavior and backend telemetry to ensure that failed requests do not partially progress downstream.

A third quality objective is measurable user experience performance under the specified network constraint. The 2.0-second end-to-end SLA under standard 4G conditions can only be trusted if the timing model includes transport, gateway processing, malware inspection, storage commit, cache invalidation, and public profile availability. The strategy will therefore measure elapsed time across the full chain rather than just API response time. Additional quality goals include deterministic repeatability in CI/CD, transparent defect traceability to requirements, and operational readiness for regression protection as the avatar flow evolves. Collectively, these objectives ensure that the release decision is grounded in evidence of security, correctness, integration integrity, synchronization accuracy, and time-bound user-visible completion.

### 3.2 Test Scope

#### 3.2.1 In Scope
- Authentication-based access gating for avatar modification UI.
- Session validation behavior for upload authorization.
- Gateway-level rejection of unauthorized requests using HTTP 401 Unauthorized.
- Single avatar image upload flow.
- Avatar image cropping capability as part of the in-scope update workflow.
- API Gateway routing and request filtering in the upload pipeline.
- Server-side image ingestion workflow.
- Synchronous inline malware scanning before persistence.
- Magic-number and file-header validation.
- File size validation up to 5MB, including exact 5.00MB acceptance and 5.01MB rejection.
- JPEG and WebP format validation.
- Storage of validated avatar images in authorized cloud buckets.
- Immediate CDN cache invalidation after successful upload.
- Immediate avatar synchronization across active views and header bars.
- Dynamic DOM-based UI refresh without full page reload.
- End-to-end performance validation under 4G network constraints.

#### 3.2.2 Out of Scope
- Client-side compression tools, because the architecture explicitly requires server-side-only processing.
- Local image scaling engines, because no browser-side transformation is permitted in this release scope.
- Front-end canvas manipulation scripts, because client image processing is excluded by design.
- PNG, SVG, GIF, and raw design file support, because the whitelist is restricted to JPEG and WebP only.
- Concurrent batch uploads, because this release supports single-avatar upload only.
- Multi-image profile header adjustments, because only one avatar update path is approved.
- Storage infrastructures outside the authorized cloud ecosystem, because they are not supported deployment targets.
- Deployment targets outside the authorized cloud ecosystem, because environmental compliance boundaries are fixed.

## 4. Test Approach

### 4.1 Test Levels and Test Types
The test approach is intentionally layered so that failures can be localized quickly while preserving end-to-end accountability. UI-level coverage validates that avatar controls are hidden or disabled when authentication is absent or expired, and visible and interactive only when the browser session contains a fresh valid token. Backend and gateway-level coverage validates authorization enforcement, upload routing, file-size boundaries, type validation, and response contract behavior. Security-layer coverage validates synchronous malware inspection and content-header analysis before any persistence event. Storage and CDN integration coverage proves that only validated content is committed to the authorized bucket path and that successful persistence triggers the expected cache invalidation sequence. Finally, end-to-end and performance layers confirm that the complete workflow produces a visible avatar update across active views within the SLA under standard 4G conditions.

This multi-level model is selected because the feature’s highest risks occur at service boundaries. A UI-only strategy would miss gateway rejection details, and an API-only strategy would miss user-facing dynamic rendering and no-reload behavior. Accordingly, the execution model includes both isolated service validation and chained transaction validation. Test design techniques include equivalence partitioning, boundary value analysis, decision-table coverage, state-transition testing, cause-effect graphing, negative testing, contract validation, structural header verification, and workflow integration matrix coverage. These techniques are mapped directly to the acceptance criteria and used to ensure that all high-risk combinations of token state, file size, file type, header match, malware result, and persistence eligibility are covered in a predictable and reviewable manner.

#### 4.1.1 Test Types Matrix
Test Type | Objective | Level | Tool / Framework | Execution Strategy | Exit Evidence
---|---|---|---|---|---
Functional Testing | Validate authenticated avatar upload and crop flow works as specified. | UI, API, E2E | Playwright | Execute authenticated upload journeys, crop interaction checks, and successful render validation across profile and header surfaces. | Passed E2E runs, screenshots, DOM assertions, API traces.
Authentication and Authorization Testing | Confirm hidden/disabled controls for invalid sessions and 401 rejections at gateway. | UI, API, Gateway | Playwright, PyTest | Combine browser-state token manipulation with API request validation for valid, expired, and absent session states. | Access-control evidence, 401 logs, negative test reports.
Boundary Value Testing | Verify 5.00MB acceptance and 5.01MB rejection behavior. | API, Gateway, E2E | PyTest, Playwright | Generate boundary-specific payloads and validate gateway enforcement plus UI banner handling. | Size threshold evidence, response logs, assertion reports.
File Format and Content Validation Testing | Verify only JPEG and WebP are accepted and magic-number checks reject spoofed files. | API, Security, E2E | PyTest | Submit supported, unsupported, and renamed payloads with structural header assertions and backend log review. | Header-validation logs, reject-path evidence, storage non-event proof.
Security Testing | Validate synchronous malware inspection prevents unsafe persistence. | Security, Storage, Gateway | PyTest, OWASP ZAP | Orchestrate approved safe-malware simulation cases, validate synchronous blocking, and inspect storage/CDN non-events. | Scan logs, blocked persistence proof, security test report.
Integration Testing | Verify gateway -> scan -> storage -> CDN invalidation chain. | Integration | PyTest, Playwright | Validate service handoffs with observability correlation IDs and post-upload synchronization checks. | Correlated event traces, integration execution logs.
End-to-End Regression Testing | Protect core upload behavior during code changes. | E2E | Playwright, PyTest | Run smoke-on-PR and nightly regression packs tied to acceptance criteria and critical paths. | CI reports, trend dashboard, regression pass record.
UI Synchronization and Dynamic Rendering Testing | Confirm avatar updates across active views with no full page reload. | UI, E2E | Playwright | Assert DOM updates, cache-busted asset visibility, and navigation timing without page refresh. | DOM mutation evidence, network logs, browser traces.
Performance / SLA Latency Testing | Measure complete transaction under standard 4G conditions within 2.0 seconds. | Performance, E2E | JMeter, tcconfig, Playwright | Apply network throttling, drive upload volume consistent with single-user SLA checks, and measure end-to-end elapsed time. | Timing reports, percentile metrics, SLA compliance summary.

### 4.2 Test Design Techniques
The test design model derives directly from the previously supplied technical approach. Equivalence partitioning will segment authentication into valid, expired, and absent token classes; content formats into supported JPEG/WebP versus excluded formats; and security states into clean, spoofed, and malware-flagged payload categories. Boundary value analysis will focus on file-size thresholds immediately below 5MB, exactly 5.00MB, and just above the threshold at 5.01MB. Decision tables will combine token validity, file-size validity, file-type allow-listing, header match, malware result, and persistence eligibility to prevent untested interaction combinations. State transition testing will validate the UI lifecycle from hidden or disabled controls to enabled authenticated behavior and the processing lifecycle from selected to validated, scanned, stored, invalidated, and rendered.

Cause-effect graphing will map gateway and validation decisions to browser banner behavior, status codes, and downstream side effects. Structural header verification will specifically confirm that the system uses actual content signatures rather than extension labels, which is essential for spoofed file interception. Workflow integration matrix coverage will connect each service handoff to the expected next state and corresponding observability artifact. The result is a strategy that is not merely broad, but analytically complete enough to support release risk decisions and defect triage with precision.

### 4.3 Test Management
Test management will be operated as a controlled engineering function rather than an ad hoc execution activity. All planned scenarios will be linked to the issue identifier AD-488 and decomposed into requirement-aligned test conditions covering authentication gating, upload success, reject conditions, storage validation, CDN invalidation, UI synchronization, and SLA timing. The master test inventory will be managed in Jira using a clear hierarchy of Epic or Story to Test task, defect, and execution evidence links. Each test case or automation asset will carry explicit references to the acceptance criteria and to the technical level it validates, such as UI, gateway, security, integration, storage, or performance. This structure ensures that when a failure occurs, the release board can immediately determine whether the defect is isolated, systemic, or likely to affect neighboring controls.

Operationally, the team will run a layered cadence. On every pull request, a lightweight smoke subset will execute automatically, including authenticated UI visibility, unauthorized gateway rejection, supported-format upload, and a representative no-reload render check. On merge to the integration branch, an expanded suite will execute including boundary files, spoofed headers, malware gate simulations, storage verification hooks, and CDN invalidation assertions. Nightly jobs will run the broader regression set with environment-health validation and artifact retention. Jira workflow states will move from Ready for QA to In Test, Blocked, Failed, Passed, or Deferred, with mandatory linkage to execution evidence and environment information. Any blocked item must identify the dependency owner and estimated recovery time.

Peer review is mandatory for all test assets and result interpretation tied to release-governing scenarios. New automation scripts must be submitted through pull request with at least one QA approver and, for gateway or security assertions, one engineering or platform approver when log queries or service mocks are modified. Test management also includes entry and exit governance: entry requires stable deployable build, seeded data availability, token-state controls, and observability readiness; exit requires zero open critical defects on in-scope controls, documented disposition for all high defects, full pass on release-blocking scenarios, and formal review of SLA evidence. This operating model ensures the strategy remains executable, auditable, and resilient to schedule pressure.

#### 4.3.2 Test Repositories
Test materials will be stored in the source control repository aligned to the application delivery workflow, with versioned folders for UI automation, API validation, performance assets, synthetic file payload generators, and supporting documentation. The repository tool will be managed under the same branch protection model as product code so that test artifacts evolve in lockstep with implementation. Playwright suites, PyTest API checks, JMeter performance plans, ZAP baseline configurations, and tcconfig network shaping scripts will be maintained as first-class assets. Test data generators for boundary-size files and spoofed content signatures will also reside in the repository to guarantee reproducibility and eliminate dependency on untracked local assets.

Tracking updates will be maintained through pull requests, commit history, linked Jira references, and release tags. Any change to release-blocking tests requires peer review, successful CI execution, and documented rationale in the pull request description. Baseline branch policies will require status checks for linting, unit validation of helpers, dry-run execution where feasible, and sign-off from designated code owners for sensitive test areas such as malware gate validation and gateway assertions. This repository discipline enables traceability from requirement to script to execution history to production-release evidence.

### 4.4 Defect Management
Defect management will be driven through Jira with a severity and priority model oriented around user impact, security exposure, and release risk. Severity 1 defects will include any condition that allows unauthenticated access to avatar controls, bypasses malware scanning, persists unsafe or spoofed content, breaks storage or CDN propagation for valid uploads, or materially breaches the 2.0-second SLA for normal operating conditions. Severity 2 defects will include inconsistent synchronization across views, unreliable boundary handling, incorrect error banner behavior, or intermittent failures in gateway validation. Every defect record must include reproducible steps, environment, build identifier, authentication state, input file characteristics, observed versus expected results, supporting screenshots or logs, and correlation IDs when available.

The workflow will progress through Open, Triaged, In Progress, Ready for QA, Retest, Verified, Deferred, or Rejected. Triaging will occur daily during active implementation and twice daily during release hardening if critical issues remain open. Root-cause categorization will distinguish UI defects, gateway configuration defects, validation-service defects, storage/CDN integration defects, observability gaps, environment defects, and test-script defects. This categorization is important because many failures in this feature can appear similar to end users while originating in very different layers. For example, a stale avatar might be caused by missing CDN invalidation, delayed event propagation, or a front-end cache issue rather than upload failure itself.

Pull-request requirements are tied directly to defect closure quality. A defect may not transition to Ready for QA unless the fixing pull request includes linked issue references, reviewer approval, passing CI checks, and an explicit statement of impacted coverage. For high-risk fixes, peer review must confirm whether adjacent regression tests were added or updated. QA verification will include both targeted retest and selective regression of neighboring controls such as token handling, header validation, or synchronization logic. Historical defect metrics will be reviewed for escape patterns including auth regressions, intermittent latency spikes, and false-negative spoof detection so the team can adjust automation investment proactively.

### 4.5 Test Data Management
#### Data Requirements
The data strategy must support realistic and intentionally adversarial coverage. Required assets include valid JPEG and WebP images across representative resolutions, files engineered to land exactly at 5.00MB, files exceeding the threshold at 5.01MB and above, excluded formats such as PNG, SVG, GIF, and raw design samples for negative coverage, renamed spoofed payloads whose extensions do not match their internal signatures, and approved malware-simulation samples that can exercise scanning controls without violating enterprise security policy. Authentication data must include user accounts with valid sessions, expired sessions, and no-session conditions. Additional seeded records are needed to verify avatar propagation across profile views and header surfaces under active authenticated sessions.

Data preparation must be centrally managed and reproducible. Sensitive or production-like user data should be masked or synthetically generated; no uncontrolled real-user avatars should be introduced into test environments. Boundary file generation should be automated through scripts so that exact byte-size control is repeatable and reviewable. Spoofed assets should be cataloged with documented original type, renamed extension, and expected rejection reason. Malware simulation assets should be stored securely with restricted access and used only in isolated environments where security teams have approved execution. This ensures the data program supports both precision and compliance.

#### Data Availability
Test Type | Data Source | Environment | Data Type | Data Volume | Comments
---|---|---|---|---|---
Functional Testing | Seeded synthetic media repository | Staging | Valid JPEG/WebP avatars | Moderate | Includes crop-capable high-resolution samples.
Authorization Testing | Auth fixture service and test accounts | Staging | Valid, expired, absent token states | Low | Must support deterministic cookie/session manipulation.
Boundary Testing | Automated file generation scripts | Staging | 4.99MB, 5.00MB, 5.01MB+ payloads | Low | Generated on demand to prevent drift.
Negative Format Testing | Seeded negative media repository | Staging | PNG, SVG, GIF, raw design samples | Low | Used to validate allow-list enforcement.
Spoof Validation Testing | Controlled spoof-data set | Staging | Renamed text/script/non-image payloads | Low | Requires header-signature mismatch documentation.
Security Testing | Restricted security-approved test asset store | Isolated Staging | Malware-simulation artifacts | Very Low | Access controlled and audited.
Performance Testing | Synthetic payload pack | Performance/Staging | Representative clean JPEG/WebP files | Moderate | Used with 4G throttling to measure SLA.

### 4.6 Test Environments
The primary environment will be a staging stack mirroring the authorized cloud ecosystem, including dedicated API Gateway routing, authentication-aware session handling, malware inspection service, object storage bucket paths, CDN invalidation hooks, and observability integrations. Browser automation nodes must support modern Chromium-family execution and capture screenshots, traces, and network logs. API test runners require secure access to gateway endpoints and service telemetry needed to confirm response contracts and downstream event behavior. The environment must also expose enough instrumentation to correlate a single upload request across gateway, scanning, storage, purge, and front-end visibility.

A secondary isolated environment is required for security-oriented execution involving malware-simulation assets and potentially disruptive network or failure-injection scenarios. Performance validation requires controlled network shaping to standard 4G conditions using tcconfig or equivalent traffic shaping mechanisms and timing capture from both client and backend perspectives. Environment readiness criteria include seeded accounts, available test data, stable endpoint configuration, storage log accessibility, and predictable CDN purge trace visibility. Any environment drift that affects SLA or synchronization evidence must be logged as a blocker before release-significant execution begins.

### 4.7 Requirement Traceability
Requirement traceability will be maintained from the original business scope through test cases, automation assets, execution runs, and defects. Each requirement extracted from Output1 and each validation dimension from Output2 will be assigned a trace identifier linked in Jira and referenced inside automation metadata where possible. For example, authentication gating requirements will map to both Playwright UI suites and PyTest gateway suites; file-size boundary requirements will map to API parameterized tests and end-to-end UI error handling checks; synchronization requirements will map to DOM mutation assertions and CDN event verification checks. This dual-layer mapping is essential because many requirements span more than one technical level and cannot be trusted if validated by only one style of test.

Operationally, the traceability model will be reviewed during refinement, test design review, and pre-release readiness meetings. Pull requests that introduce or change behavior must identify which trace items are affected and whether new tests were added. CI/CD integration will publish links from execution jobs back to requirement identifiers so release stakeholders can inspect objective-level status rather than raw test counts. Defects will also inherit requirement links, making it possible to identify coverage gaps, instability clusters, and recurring failure patterns over time. This matters especially for cross-service flows like malware scan to storage to CDN invalidation, where one defect can invalidate several business outcomes simultaneously.

Peer-review processes will reinforce this model. Reviewers of automation changes must confirm that the associated requirement references are correct, that no in-scope acceptance criterion has become orphaned, and that reject-path validation remains represented alongside success-path coverage. Requirement traceability is therefore not a static spreadsheet exercise; it is an active release-control mechanism that ensures every critical acceptance criterion remains visible from planning through execution and defect closure.

### 4.8 Test Reporting
#### 4.8.1 Reporting Cadence
Daily execution summaries will be published during active testing, with additional intraday updates for any Severity 1 or release-blocking event. Reports will summarize pass/fail counts, blocked items, environment issues, SLA observations, requirement coverage status, and newly opened or reopened defects. During code-freeze and release hardening, reporting will emphasize trend direction, not just snapshot status, so decision-makers can distinguish between transient instability and structural defects.

#### 4.8.2 Maintaining Test Results
Historical test execution logs, browser traces, screenshots, API evidence, performance reports, and security scan outputs will be retained in the CI artifact store and linked from Jira execution records and release reports. Retention should be sufficient to cover audit and rollback analysis needs for the release cycle. At minimum, all release-candidate runs and all runs linked to Severity 1 or Severity 2 defects will remain accessible through the full release validation period and immediate post-release warranty window.

### 4.9 Tools
Tool | Purpose | Integration Strategy | Owner
---|---|---|---
Playwright | UI automation for authentication gating, crop interaction, DOM refresh validation, and no-full-page-reload assertions. | Integrated into CI pipelines for pull-request smoke, merge regression, and nightly end-to-end execution with trace capture and screenshot artifacts. Playwright is selected because it can validate browser state, intercept network behavior, and assert dynamic rendering outcomes across profile and header surfaces with strong reliability. | QA Automation Team
PyTest | API, gateway, integration, and backend validation including status code checks, file boundary enforcement, header-signature validation, and storage/CDN event orchestration. | Integrated as service-level and integration suites triggered on merge and nightly schedules. PyTest is selected for parameterization, fixture management, and straightforward integration with log correlation and custom payload generation for boundary and spoof scenarios. | QA Automation Team
JMeter | Performance and SLA measurement across upload workflows under controlled conditions. | Executed in dedicated performance stages or scheduled windows with report export to CI artifacts and trend dashboards. JMeter is selected because it can model timing, throughput, and response distributions while working alongside network shaping to assess the 2.0-second objective. | Performance QA Team
OWASP ZAP | Security-oriented baseline and targeted validation of gateway exposure, request handling, and upload-path hardening. | Used in controlled security stages and as a supplementary validation layer around upload and gateway routes. ZAP is selected to provide additional confidence that request filtering and security headers remain aligned with secure deployment expectations. | Security QA Team
tcconfig | Network shaping to standard 4G conditions for SLA verification. | Applied in performance test environments before execution of JMeter or browser-driven SLA scenarios. tcconfig is selected because the acceptance criteria explicitly depend on network-constrained timing, and reproducible traffic shaping is required to make results defensible. | Performance QA Team

The selected toolchain is intentionally complementary rather than redundant. Playwright owns the user-visible truth of the feature and validates whether controls are hidden, enabled, rendered, and synchronized correctly from an end-user perspective. PyTest owns protocol-level precision and is better suited for parameterized boundary and negative validation, correlation-friendly service checks, and orchestrated backend assertions. JMeter and tcconfig together provide a defensible model for SLA verification under constrained network conditions, which pure browser timings alone would not standardize sufficiently. OWASP ZAP supplements the release by probing security posture around the upload surface and ensuring that deployment hardening does not lag behind functional success.

In CI/CD, these tools will be sequenced to maximize fast feedback and reduce unnecessary cost. Pull requests will run Playwright and PyTest smoke coverage. Merge pipelines will run broader Playwright and PyTest regression packs plus lightweight ZAP baseline checks where appropriate. Scheduled runs or release-candidate gates will invoke tcconfig-shaped environments and JMeter scenarios for SLA certification. All tools will publish machine-readable outputs and human-readable artifacts so Jira, dashboards, and release governance reports remain synchronized.

## 5. Entry and Exit Criteria
Entry criteria include an integrated deployable build in staging, stable API Gateway configuration, available authentication fixtures for valid and invalid session states, seeded test data sets including boundary and spoofed files, accessible malware-scan and storage observability, and approved environment readiness for 4G network shaping. Exit criteria include successful execution of all release-blocking scenarios, zero open Severity 1 defects, approved disposition for any remaining Severity 2 defects, demonstrated compliance with authentication, validation, storage, synchronization, and no-reload behaviors, and objective evidence that the end-to-end transaction meets the 2.0-second SLA under standard 4G conditions.

## 6. Test Schedule / Milestone
A high-level schedule estimate for AD-488 is as follows. Day 1 to Day 2: test analysis finalization, traceability setup, and test data generation for valid, boundary, spoofed, and malware-simulation payloads. Day 3 to Day 5: Playwright UI automation development for auth gating, upload flow, crop checks, and synchronization behavior; parallel PyTest development for gateway, validation, and storage/CDN integration assertions. Day 6: peer review, CI pipeline integration, and dry-run stabilization. Day 7: functional and negative regression execution in staging. Day 8: performance and SLA execution using JMeter with tcconfig under standard 4G shaping. Day 9: defect retest, focused regression, and release evidence consolidation. Day 10: Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 review, publication readiness, and release recommendation.

Core resource alignment is estimated as one QA lead/test manager, one UI automation engineer, one API/integration automation engineer, one performance engineer shared part-time, one security engineer shared part-time for malware and gateway hardening checks, and fractional support from a DevOps/platform engineer for observability and environment controls. This staffing model is intentionally lean but sufficient because the scope is narrow to a single workflow while still requiring cross-layer verification. Timeline risk increases materially if observability access, malware-simulation approval, or CDN traceability are delayed, so these dependencies should be secured before automation development begins.

## 7. Roles and Responsibilities
The QA Lead owns strategy governance, traceability completeness, defect triage leadership, and final release recommendation. The UI Automation Engineer owns Playwright implementation, browser evidence quality, and synchronization/no-reload assertions. The API/Integration Automation Engineer owns PyTest service checks, gateway validation, header-signature testing, and storage/CDN event verification logic. The Performance Engineer owns JMeter planning, execution, and SLA interpretation under tcconfig-shaped conditions. The Security Engineer owns malware-simulation coordination, ZAP usage, and review of security-oriented findings. DevOps or Platform support owns environment readiness, observability access, artifact retention plumbing, and CI/CD execution reliability.

## 8. Communication and Governance
Testing status will be communicated through daily stand-ups, Jira dashboards, execution summaries, and pre-release readiness reviews. Any discovery that affects authentication enforcement, malware gating, storage safety, or SLA compliance will be escalated immediately as a release risk. Governance includes mandatory peer review for test assets, branch protections for repository changes, and a formal Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 checkpoint where execution state is set to Paused pending manual validation approval before final release recommendation is enacted.

## 9. Risk Mitigation Table
Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Severity | Mitigation Actions | Contingency Owner
---|---|---|---|---|---|---
R1 | Authentication token expiration handling may differ between browser UI state and gateway validation, causing the upload control to appear enabled while backend rejects the request. | Medium | High | High | 1) Implement paired UI and API tests for valid, expired, and absent tokens. 2) Add pre-merge checks validating cookie/session freshness logic. 3) Require telemetry correlation between front-end auth state and gateway response codes. 4) Add targeted regression whenever session middleware changes. | QA Lead / Application Engineer
R2 | Dynamic database or session-backed profile metadata connection drops during upload processing may prevent avatar reference updates even when the image is stored successfully. | Medium | High | High | 1) Add integration checks that validate post-storage profile reference persistence and retrieval. 2) Capture correlation IDs across storage and profile update services. 3) Introduce fault-observation monitoring for metadata write failures. 4) Define rollback or compensating cleanup for orphaned stored assets. | Backend Engineer / QA Integration Engineer
R3 | Synchronous malware scanning latency may cause end-to-end SLA breaches under standard 4G conditions, especially for near-limit files. | High | High | Critical | 1) Benchmark scan durations with representative 5.00MB payloads. 2) Execute JMeter and Playwright timing runs under tcconfig-shaped 4G conditions. 3) Establish alert thresholds for scan-stage latency contribution. 4) Optimize scan configuration or infrastructure sizing before release sign-off if latency variance trends upward. | Performance Engineer / Security Engineer
R4 | Mock or lower-environment CDN invalidation desynchronization may falsely indicate stale avatars or hide real production timing risks. | Medium | High | High | 1) Validate purge trigger logs and front-end fetch behavior together, not independently. 2) Use cache-busting evidence and DOM assertions across active sessions. 3) Compare lower-environment invalidation timings against expected production baselines. 4) Gate release on successful multi-surface synchronization evidence rather than upload success alone. | DevOps / UI Automation Engineer
R5 | Magic-number validation rules may be inconsistently implemented across gateway and validation service, allowing spoofed renamed files to progress deeper into the pipeline. | Medium | High | High | 1) Maintain a controlled spoofed file suite covering mismatched extensions and header signatures. 2) Validate reject behavior at both gateway response and backend log levels. 3) Confirm no storage or CDN event occurs after header mismatch. 4) Add regression coverage whenever allow-list logic or upload middleware changes. | Security QA / Backend Engineer
R6 | Observability gaps across gateway, malware scan, storage, and CDN layers may prevent accurate root-cause analysis and delay release decisions. | Medium | Medium | Medium | 1) Define mandatory correlation fields before test execution starts. 2) Verify log access and artifact retention as an entry criterion. 3) Create execution dashboards linking service events to test runs. 4) Escalate any missing telemetry as an environment blocker, not a test script issue. | DevOps / QA Lead
R7 | Timeline compression caused by late environment readiness or approval delays at Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 may reduce regression depth before release. | Medium | High | High | 1) Front-load smoke automation and boundary suites first. 2) Lock environment and approval dates by milestone Day 6. 3) Use risk-based execution ordering centered on auth, security, storage, synchronization, and SLA controls. 4) Preserve a minimum release-blocking regression pack that cannot be waived without director approval. | QA Lead / Release Manager

